  "summary": "Ottoman art of inlaid patterns of iridescent mother-of-pearl set into dark walnut or ebony woodwork.",
  "description": "Sedefkari is the Ottoman craft of cutting thin discs and splinters of mother-of-pearl (sedef) and inlaying them flush into dark woods — walnut, ebony, sometimes rosewood — often wired with brass or silver to form tulips, carnations, interlaced stars and calligraphic medallions. Istanbul and Edirne workshops fitted palace doors, Qur'an cases, turban stands, writing boxes and the interiors of waterside yalı mansions. The craft survived the empire's end in small ateliers and remains a protected Turkish traditional handicraft today.",
